  "articleBody": "Renovating a foreclosed property can be a fantastic investment, but it's easy for costs to spiral if you're not careful. One of the biggest mistakes we see is underestimating hidden damage. You might see a great deal, but what you don't see—like old electrical systems, plumbing failures, or unnoticed structural issues—can quickly eat into your budget. That's why skipping contingency reserves is a huge no-no; always budget an extra 10% to 25% of the purchase price for those unexpected surprises. Another common pitfall is doing cosmetic work, like new paint or flooring, before resolving water intrusion or major system failures. You’ll just end up redoing it all! It's also vital to align your renovation scope with your end goal, whether that's reselling for profit or renting it out. Don't over-renovate for the neighborhood, and don't assume every foreclosed home just needs a light facelift. Many require substantial system repairs to make them safe and marketable. We can help you walk through the property and identify those potential budget-busters early on, ensuring your renovation is smart and sustainable.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
